{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_smmyssk2wmeosx447czlgwykaf7umbd34iyv2hgzrxq6niflyfla","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Verdict\n\nI audited all 11 items statement-by-statement and then **verified by differential execution**: I compiled the C with gcc (`-O2`, asserts live) and the Rust with `rustc` into two `.so`s, `dlopen`ed both into one process, and compared results plus full internal state (array header, `stbds_hash_index` fields, every bucket's `hash[]`/`index[]`, element bytes, arena state) over ~1M operations. Everything matched byte-for-byte:\n\n```\nOK   hash_bytes              lens 0..48 x 6 seeds x 300 buffers + exhaustive single-byte sweeps\nOK   hash_string             5000 random strings (incl. 0x80..0xff chars) + single-char sweep\nOK   arrgrowf/arrfreef       5399 bytes of state dumps identical (incl. size_t-wrap cases)\nOK   hmput_default           155 bytes identical\nOK   hashmap-binary          256435 bytes identical (grow/shrink/rebuild, all bucket contents)\nOK   hashmap-string-dflt     269774 bytes identical (incl. temp_key)\nOK   hashmap-strdup          248174 bytes identical\nOK   hashmap-arena           248608 bytes identical\nOK   stralloc/strreset       7136 bytes identical\nOK   seed chain identical for 4 starting seeds x 64 make_hash_index(ot=NULL) steps\n```\n\nI also built the Rust with `-C overflow-checks=on -C debug-assertions=on -C opt-level=0` (the debug profile) and re-ran the workloads: **no panic** — none of the non-`wrapping_*` arithmetic in the audited functions is reachable at an overflowing value.\n\n## Divergences\n\n**1. `stbds_make_hash_index` | C 401 | Rust 341 | omitted assertion**\n- C: `STBDS_ASSERT(t->used_count_threshold + t->tombstone_count_threshold < t->slot_count);` — with no `NDEBUG` this is a live `assert()` that calls `abort()` when violated.\n- Rust: replaced by the comment `// STBDS_ASSERT(used_count_threshold + tombstone_count_threshold < slot_count)`; no check at all.\n- Distinguishing input: needs `slot_count` ∈ {0, 1, 2, 4} (e.g. `slot_count == 0` → `0 + 0 < 0` is false → C aborts, Rust returns a table). **Not reachable via the public API**: the only call sites pass `STBDS_BUCKET_LENGTH` (8), `table->slot_count*2`, or `table->slot_count>>1` guarded by `slot_count > STBDS_BUCKET_LENGTH`, so `slot_count` is always a power of two ≥ 8, for which `0.75n + 0.1875n < n` always holds. This is the only textual divergence I found in the audited set.\n\n*(Note: the same pattern — `STBDS_ASSERT` dropped entirely — also holds for C 778/828/832/846/849/913, which are outside my audit scope. Those **are** reachable: with a deliberately misused `STBDS_SH_DEFAULT` map whose key strings are freed/overwritten, the C aborts at `lib.c:846 Assertion 'slot >= 0' failed` while the Rust silently continues. Correct API use does not trigger them.)*\n\n## Benign differences (no observable divergence)\n\n**`stbds_make_hash_index` | C 404 | Rust 344-349 | struct padding.** C `t->string = ot->string;` is a whole-object copy (gcc copies all 24 bytes, including the 6 padding bytes after `mode`); Rust builds a fresh `stbds_string_arena` literal field-by-field, leaving those 6 padding bytes unspecified. No code in either version reads the padding (the `ot == NULL` path `memset`s all 24 bytes in both), so it is unobservable.\n\n**`stbds_arrgrowf` | C 278, 281 | — | dead code.** The Rust omits `stbds_array_header temp={0};` and `(void) sizeof(temp);`. No effect.\n\n**`STBDS_ROTATE_LEFT/RIGHT`, `STBDS_ALIGN_FWD`, `stbds_load_32_or_64` | generality only.** The C versions are macros (multiple evaluation of `val`/`n`; `~((a)-1)` is a sign-extended `int`; `v32`/`v64_lo` carry their literals' C types). The Rust versions are functions with fixed `u32`/`usize` parameters. At every existing call site the arguments are side-effect-free variables and non-negative constants that fit the Rust parameter types, so results are identical — I confirmed numerically: `a = 0x27bb2ee687b0b0fd`, `b = 0xb504f32d`, `align_fwd(1|64|65|0, 64) = 64|64|128|0` in both. These are not equivalent *as general macros*, only at the instantiations present.\n\n## Per-item results\n\n| Item | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `stbds_arrgrowf` (C 276-310 / Rust 213-256) | Exactly equivalent, including `ptrdiff_t + size_t → size_t` for `min_len`, `2*arrcap` wrap, `elemsize*min_cap + 32` wrap (verified: both produce `cap = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF` and a successful 16-byte `realloc` for the wrapping case), the `a == NULL` field init, and the `arrgrowf(NULL,es,0,0) → NULL` early return. |\n| `stbds_arrfreef` (C 312-315 / Rust 258-263) | Exactly equivalent (`free(a - 32)`; `wrapping_sub` matches for `a == NULL`). |\n| `stbds_rand_seed` + `stbds_hash_seed` (C 353-358 / Rust 269-276) | Exactly equivalent; initial value `0x31415926`, internal linkage in both. |\n| `stbds_probe_position` (C 367-373 / Rust 282-284) | Exactly equivalent (`hash & (slot_count-1)`, `slot_log2` unused; matches even for `slot_count == 0`). |\n| `stbds_log2` (C 375-383 / Rust 286-294) | Exactly equivalent (`0,1,8,9,SIZE_MAX → 0,0,3,3,63` in both); `n += 1` cannot overflow. |\n| `stbds_load_32_or_64` (C 360-363 / Rust 296-312) | Equivalent at both instantiations (see note above). |\n| `stbds_make_hash_index` (C 385-472 / Rust 314-432) | Equivalent apart from the missing assert. Loop bounds, `goto done` ≡ `break 'done`, per-item `step` reset, `pos &= slot_count-1`, seed advance, `temp_key` deliberately left uninitialised in both. |\n| `stbds_hash_string` (C 477-491 / Rust 438-457) | Exactly equivalent, including the two no-op `hash ^= hash ^ ROR(...)` idioms and unsigned-char promotion. |\n| `stbds_siphash_bytes` (C 498-551 / Rust 485-580) | Exactly equivalent. The sign-extension quirks are reproduced at **every** site: main loop `d[0]|…|(d[3]<<24)` → `(lo as i32) as usize`; the `(size_t)(…)<<16<<16` high half (where sign extension is shifted out) → identical bit pattern; tail cases 7/6/5 as `usize` shifts by 48/40/32; **case 4 `data |= (d[3] << 24)` → `(((d3 as u32) << 24) as i32) as usize`, i.e. bits 32-63 set when `d[3] >= 0x80`** — matches gcc. Cases 3/2/1 stay non-negative. Fall-through 7→1 reproduced by the descending `if rem >= n` chain; `len - i` is always 0..7 so the `rem >= 8` behavioural difference (C `switch` does nothing, Rust would run case 7) is unreachable. Verified by exhaustive single-byte sweeps for `len` 1..16 at every position and value 0..255. |\n| `stbds_hash_bytes` (C 553-556 / Rust 582-585) | Exactly equivalent. |\n| `stbds_is_key_equal` (C 558-564 / Rust 591-612) | Exactly equivalent, including passing a `ptrdiff_t` index through as `size_t`/`usize` (same bit pattern). |\n| `stbds_hmfree_func` (C 571-584 / Rust 618-637) | Exactly equivalent (loop from 1, re-read of `length`, `strreset` then `free(hash_table)` then `free(header)`). |\n| `stbds_hm_find_slot` (C 586-629 / Rust 643-720) | Exactly equivalent, including `(pos & ~STBDS_BUCKET_MASK)+i` → `isize`, the two half-bucket scans, and the unbounded probe loop. |\n| Struct layouts (C 227-247, 325-344) | Identical: `array_header` 32/align 8/offsets 0,8,16,24; `string_block` 16/0,8; `string_arena` 24/0,8,16,17; `hash_bucket` 128/0,64; `hash_index` 104/0,8,16,24,32,40,48,56,64,72,96 — measured on both sides. |\n| Constants | `STBDS_BUCKET_LENGTH=8`, `SHIFT=3`, `MASK=7`, `CACHE_LINE_SIZE=64`, `INDEX_EMPTY=-1`, `INDEX_DELETED=-2`, `HASH_EMPTY=0`, `HASH_DELETED=1`, `SIZE_T_BITS=64`, `HDR_SIZE=32` — all identical. |\n\nRelevant paths: `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-Tzy9eC/c_src/src/lib.c`, `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-Tzy9eC/translation/src/lib.rs`. No files were modified."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3580,"cache_read_input_tokens":121647,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3580,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01U5FKuMYMoxyFeazqLGHn7p","session_id":"c378fbc5-cb29-4f2d-9c80-7b97c2b5be7b","uuid":"e4109e1c-40f7-4b12-ac7e-496ec5e951ce","timestamp":"2026-09-05T06:13:20.123Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit C vs Rust: hash + array core"}
