{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_5n6y55d6abmocq7pjpewz4qrn77qseavvdwceqlys6twhwmi5ubq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_017VJpKEMLL3Vq6m4S69FPng","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'PY'\np='ERRORS.md'; s=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace(\"| `err_34_stralloc_block_saturation` | [x] |\",\n            \"| `err_34_stralloc_block_saturation` + `err_34b_stralloc_block_full_range` (all 29 boundary values of the `unsigned char`, each in a subprocess) | [x] |\")\ns=s.replace('''## The one divergence verification found''','''## The two divergences verification found\n\n### 2. `stbds_stralloc` shift count (`lib.c:888`)\n\n`a->block` is an `unsigned char` and `stbds_stralloc` is **exported**, so a caller\ncan pass any value 0..=255 and the C's shift count `a->block >> 1` reaches 127.\ngcc emits `shlq %cl`, which masks the count to 6 bits. The Rust used a plain\n`<<`, which is fine in the release profile (`overflow-checks = false`) but\n**panics and aborts** in the dev profile for any count >= 64:\n\n```\na->block = 128   C            -> \"hello\", block=129, remaining=506  (exit 0)\n                 Rust release -> identical\n                 Rust debug   -> SIGABRT (\"attempt to shift left with overflow\")\na->block = 255   C            -> \"hello\", block=0,   remaining=0    (exit 0)\n                 Rust debug   -> SIGABRT\n```\n\nFixed with `wrapping_shl`, which masks the count the same way. Nine other plain\n`+`/`-` sites reachable from caller-poked state were made `wrapping_*` for the\nsame reason. Verified with a negative control (restoring the plain `<<` makes\n`err_34b_stralloc_block_full_range` fail at `block = 128`).\n\nFor `a->block` in {63, 64, 100, 200} the C's *unchecked* `realloc` (row 4) returns\nNULL and the C stores through it: C and release-Rust both SIGSEGV; the dev\nprofile's null-pointer check traps the same UB one step earlier and aborts. The\ntest requires an exact match whenever the C returns normally, and \"both died\" on\nthe row-4 UB inputs.\n\n### 1. The dropped `STBDS_ASSERT`s''')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\n\np='VERIFICATION.md'; s=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace('## The divergence that was found and fixed','## The two divergences that were found and fixed\\n\\n### 1. All 7 `STBDS_ASSERT`s were dropped')\ns=s.replace('''Apart from that, `src/lib.rs` needed no changes.''','''### 2. `stbds_stralloc`'s shift count (`lib.c:888`)\n\n`a->block` is an `unsigned char` and `stbds_stralloc` is exported, so the C's\nshift count `a->block >> 1` can reach 127. gcc emits `shlq %cl`, masking it to 6\nbits. The Rust's plain `<<` is fine in the release profile\n(`overflow-checks = false`) but **panics/aborts in the dev profile** for any count\n>= 64:\n\n```\na->block = 128   C -> \"hello\", block=129, remaining=506 (exit 0)\n                 Rust release -> identical\n                 Rust debug   -> SIGABRT (attempt to shift left with overflow)\na->block = 255   C -> \"hello\", block=0, remaining=0 (exit 0)\n                 Rust debug   -> SIGABRT\n```\n\n**Fix:** `wrapping_shl`, whose count is masked with `& 63` on a 64-bit target,\nexactly like `shlq %cl`. The same reasoning applies to every other plain\n`+`/`-` on a value a caller can control, so nine further sites were switched to\n`wrapping_*`: the `sizeof(*sb)-8+len` allocations in `stbds_stralloc`,\n`header->length -= 1` in `stbds_hmdel_key`, the three `header->length += 1`,\n`i+1`/`i-1` in `stbds_hmput_key`'s `found_empty_slot`, `arrlen-1-1` in\n`stbds_hmdel_key`, and `idx+1` in `arrpush_int`. Loop counters,\n`rotate_left`/`rotate_right` (always non-zero constant counts < 64) and the\nconstant `len << 56` were deliberately left alone.\n\n`err_34b_stralloc_block_full_range` pins all 29 boundary values of `a->block`,\neach in a subprocess. Negative control: restoring the plain `<<` makes it fail at\n`block = 128`.\n\nFor `a->block` in {63, 64, 100, 200} the C's **unchecked** `realloc` (non-goal 1\nbelow) returns NULL and the C stores through it — C and release-Rust both\nSIGSEGV, while the dev profile's null-pointer check traps the same UB one step\nearlier and aborts. The test demands an exact match whenever the C returns\nnormally, and only \"both died\" on those row-4 UB inputs.\n\nApart from these two, `src/lib.rs` needed no changes.''')\ns=s.replace('* **Totals** — 85 test functions','* **Totals** — 87 test functions')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nPY\ngrep -n 'divergence' ERRORS.md VERIFICATION.md | head","description":"Document the second divergence in the artifacts"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":790,"cache_read_input_tokens":378103,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":790,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c378fbc5-cb29-4f2d-9c80-7b97c2b5be7b","uuid":"9350e1f6-3f94-417a-9e91-81ea39feac69","timestamp":"2026-09-05T06:35:46.364Z"}
